[1mAddresses[0m
       [4mSed[24m commands can be given with no addresses, in which case the command will be executed for all input lines; with one address, in which case the command will only be executed for
       input lines which match that address; or with two addresses, in which case the command will be executed for all input lines which match the inclusive range of lines starting from
       the  first address and continuing to the second address.  Three things to note about address ranges: the syntax is [4maddr1[24m,[4maddr2[24m (i.e., the addresses are separated by a comma); the
       line which [4maddr1[24m matched will always be accepted, even if [4maddr2[24m selects an earlier line; and if [4maddr2[24m is a [4mregexp[24m, it will not be tested against the line that [4maddr1[24m matched.

       After the address (or address-range), and before the command, a [1m!  [22mmay be inserted, which specifies that the command shall only be executed if the address (or address-range) does
       [1mnot [22mmatch.

       The following address types are supported:

       [4mnumber[24m Match only the specified line [4mnumber[24m (which increments cumulatively across files, unless the [1m-s [22moption is specified on the command line).

       [4mfirst[24m~[4mstep[0m
              Match every [4mstep[24m'th line starting with line [4mfirst[24m.  For example, ``sed -n 1~2p'' will print all the odd-numbered lines in the input stream, and the address 2~5 will  match
              every fifth line, starting with the second.  [4mfirst[24m can be zero; in this case, [4msed[24m operates as if it were equal to [4mstep[24m.  (This is an extension.)

       $      Match the last line.

       /[4mregexp[24m/
              Match lines matching the regular expression [4mregexp[24m.  Matching is performed on the current pattern space, which can be modified with commands such as ``s///''.

       \[1mc[4m[22mregexp[24m[1mc[0m
              Match lines matching the regular expression [4mregexp[24m.  The [1mc [22mmay be any character.

       GNU [4msed[24m also supports some special 2-address forms:

       0,[4maddr2[0m
              Start  out  in "matched first address" state, until [4maddr2[24m is found.  This is similar to 1,[4maddr2[24m, except that if [4maddr2[24m matches the very first line of input the 0,[4maddr2[24m form
              will be at the end of its range, whereas the 1,[4maddr2[24m form will still be at the beginning of its range.  This works only when [4maddr2[24m is a regular expression.

       [4maddr1[24m,+[4mN[0m
              Will match [4maddr1[24m and the [4mN[24m lines following [4maddr1[24m.

       [4maddr1[24m,~[4mN[0m
              Will match [4maddr1[24m and the lines following [4maddr1[24m until the next line whose input line number is a multiple of [4mN[24m.

[1mREGULAR EXPRESSIONS[0m
       POSIX.2 BREs [4mshould[24m be supported, but they aren't completely because of performance problems.  The [1m\n [22msequence in a regular expression matches the newline  character,  and  simi‐
       larly  for  [1m\a[22m, [1m\t[22m, and other sequences.  The [4m-E[24m option switches to using extended regular expressions instead; it has been supported for years by GNU sed, and is now included in
       POSIX.

